## Authentication & Configuration

Two auth modes, selected per-API by whether a token is available. Both modes are
production paths.

### Mode A — WebView bridge tokens (HC-002)

1. Flutter loads the dashboard URL with non-sensitive config as URL parameters.
2. `src/lib/api/config.ts` parses parameters; any `p1_token` / `sungrow_token`
   present in the URL (dev/test fallback only) are extracted and **immediately
   scrubbed via `history.replaceState`**, before validation.
3. Flutter posts `{type: "bootstrap", p1_token, sungrow_token}` (and later
   `token_refresh`) via `postMessage`. Origin allowlist: same-origin, plus
   `'null'` only when `window.flutter_inappwebview` is present. Payloads are
   schema-checked before use.
4. Tokens live in JS memory only. Never in localStorage, sessionStorage,
   cookies, or logs. Requests carry `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.

### Mode B — Proxy mode (cookie auth)

Implemented 2026-03-01, documented here for the first time (ADR-009). When no
token is supplied for an API, the client sends requests with
`credentials: 'include'`; a Caddy reverse proxy in front of the APIs
authenticates the browser session cookie and injects the backend Bearer token
server-side. The dashboard never sees a token in this mode.

**Security consequence — base-URL allowlist (mandatory):** in proxy mode a
crafted `p1_base`/`sungrow_base` URL parameter would make the browser send
credentialed requests to an attacker's host; in bridge mode it would leak the
Bearer token in the `Authorization` header. Checking for an `https://` prefix —
all the legacy `config.js` does — is **insufficient**. `validate.ts` must parse
each base URL and verify the host against a build-time allowlist of first-party
API hosts (the same set templated into the CSP via `CSP_CONNECT_HOSTS`), rejecting
anything else with a config error. CSP `connect-src` is the backstop, not the
defense.

### Configuration parameters

| Parameter | Purpose | Delivery | Required |
|-----------|---------|----------|----------|
| `p1_base` / `sungrow_base` | API base URLs | URL parameter — **allowlist-validated** | Yes |
| `p1_device_id` / `sungrow_device_id` | Device identifiers | URL parameter | Yes |
| `p1_token` / `sungrow_token` | Bearer tokens | postMessage bridge (URL fallback scrubbed) | No — absence selects proxy mode per API |
| `mock` | Mock data mode | URL parameter (`true`/`false`) | No (default `false`) |

Outbound bridge: `window.flutter_inappwebview.callHandler('onEvent', data)`;
the app dispatches `dashboardReady` after mount. No theme channel (ADR-004).

### Input Validation (hard rules)

| Input | Validation |
|-------|-----------|
| API JSON responses | Schema guards in `validate.ts` (types, null-safety, range sanity) before data enters the store; malformed responses count as fetch failures |
| URL parameters | Type + format + **host allowlist** for base URLs; allowlist for known param names; unknown params ignored |
| postMessage events | Origin allowlist + type/schema check |
| Timer callbacks | Stale-data detection; errors contained per source, never crash the UI |
| Rendered values | React's default escaping; no `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` for data-derived content |

