## State Management & Polling

No global state library. Server data lives in the data layer behind
`useEnergyData()`; UI-local state stays in components. If the Lovable output
includes TanStack Query, the data layer may be implemented on it
(`refetchInterval` + `refetchIntervalInBackground: false` supplies visibility
gating natively) — **to be confirmed against the delivered lockfile**; otherwise a
hand-rolled scheduler module (pure, injectable clock, unit-testable) implements
the same contract. Either way the following behavior is normative:

### Poll cadence (unchanged)

| Data | Interval |
|------|----------|
| Realtime (P1 + Sungrow, parallel) | 5 s |
| Energy balance (Sungrow series day) | 60 s |
| Timeline (Sungrow series day) | 5 min |
| Monthly overview (Sungrow series month) | on load |
| Capacity month | on load + month rollover |

### Visibility-gated polling (new — fixes a live defect)

The legacy app polls forever, including while the WebView is backgrounded,
burning battery and data. The new data layer **must** subscribe to
`document.visibilitychange`: on hidden, all polling pauses; on visible, an
immediate refresh of every feed fires and intervals restart. The Flutter host
backgrounding the WebView must produce zero network activity.

### Failure backoff (new — fixes a live defect)

The legacy app retries every 5 s indefinitely against a dead API. The new client
applies per-source exponential backoff on consecutive failures: 5 s → 10 s →
20 s → 40 s, capped at 60 s; any success resets to the base cadence. Staleness
badges and the offline banner (both sources ≥ 3 consecutive failures) reflect
state per HC-003. Fetch timeout stays at 30 s via `AbortController`.

### Caching

Last-known values per feed are retained in memory with the timestamp of last
success; failures serve the cached value flagged stale (HC-003). No persistent
storage — state resets on load.

