### ADR-005: Adopt the Lovable-Generated React Frontend As-Is
**Status**: Approved
**Date**: 2026-07-29

**Context**: v1 (vanilla JS, single file) is functionally complete but
under-delivers the interaction spec, and iterating manual-DOM UI is slow. The
frontend was re-designed in Lovable (project "Energy Watch", stack
`tanstack_start_ts_current`): TanStack Start (React) + TypeScript + Vite +
Tailwind + shadcn/ui, Bun package manager, file-based routing. The decision to
adopt the generated screens as-is has been made by the owner.

**Decision**: The Lovable output becomes the frontend. The repo re-bases on its
project layout. Vanilla modules are frozen in `legacy/` and mined for domain
logic and tests, then deleted. HC-001 is retired; `scripts/build.js` is retired;
ADR-001 is superseded.

**Consequences**:
- A dependency tree exists; the lockfile is the approved baseline and new
  runtime deps are gated (Tech Stack §Dependency Gate).
- Bun is adopted as the package manager per the generated project. (Vite runs
  identically under Node; this is a toolchain preference, not a runtime one.)
- All hard-won domain behavior must be actively ported — sign conventions,
  degradation states, credential handling — because the generated screens were
  built against a mock layer, not the real APIs.
- Governance documents referencing the old gates (CLAUDE.md §3 commands, HC
  table) require amendment (M7).

### ADR-006: Static SPA Build — No Server Runtime in Production
**Status**: Approved
**Date**: 2026-07-29

**Context**: TanStack Start is a full-stack framework with SSR and server
functions. This app runs inside a Flutter WebView, is served today as static
files by nginx in Docker behind Caddy, and has **no backend of its own** — both
data APIs are external services the client calls directly.

**Decision**: Build as a **client-only SPA** (TanStack Start SPA/prerender mode —
exact `vite.config.ts` flag per the generated framework version, TBC). The
production artifact is static `dist/`; nginx serves it with an `index.html`
fallback. No Node/Bun process in production. Server features of the framework
are off-limits (HC-005).

**Alternatives considered**:
| Option | Pros | Cons |
|--------|------|------|
| Static SPA (chosen) | Keeps proven nginx/Docker/Caddy deploy; zero new runtime to patch/monitor; no SSR failure mode when offline; WebView has no SEO/first-paint-HTML needs; server functions have literally no job here | Blank-ish first paint until JS loads (mitigated: skeleton states are mandatory anyway) |
| Node/Bun SSR runtime | Framework-default; faster first contentful paint | New production runtime + attack surface; server becomes a single point of failure for a dashboard whose whole ethos is degrade-gracefully; nothing server-side to compute |
| SSG + hydration middle ground | Pre-rendered shell | Gains nothing over SPA-with-skeletons for an all-dynamic dashboard |

**Consequences**: deployment pipeline stays nginx-shaped (M6 updates Dockerfile/
deploy.sh for the multi-file artifact); config cannot use server-side env vars —
URL params + bridge remain the only config channels; if a server-side need ever
emerges (e.g., token exchange), that is a new ADR, not a quiet flag flip.

### ADR-007: Bundled Charting Library (No CDN Resources of Any Kind)
**Status**: Approved
**Date**: 2026-07-29
**Supersedes**: ADR-002

**Context**: The Lovable output ships its own charting (expected: Recharts via
shadcn/ui chart primitives — **to be confirmed** against the delivered code).
The CDN rationale from ADR-002 is gone with HC-001.

**Decision**: Use whatever charting library the Lovable screens are built on,
bundled from the lockfile. **No CDN-loaded scripts, styles, or fonts anywhere in
the app** — the CSP enforces this (`script-src 'self'`). If the delivered
library proves too heavy for the HC-005 budget, replacement with a lighter
bundled option is an amendment to this ADR, not a return to CDN.

**Consequences**: charts work offline (fixes a v1 degradation gap); no SRI
management; chart bundle weight is now our budget problem — measure at M3 and
code-split detail routes.

### ADR-008: Power Flow Diagram as Inline SVG in React
**Status**: Approved
**Date**: 2026-07-29
**Supersedes**: ADR-003 (restates its substance for the new stack)

**Decision**: The hero diagram remains inline SVG — now JSX-rendered: node
positions, path geometry, and dash animation as React components driven by
`computeFlows` output props. CSS/SMIL dash animation, ~3 s loops, direction-flip
flash ~300 ms, all under `prefers-reduced-motion` guards.

**Rationale (unchanged)**: retina-sharp scaling, GPU-friendly line animation,
DOM-addressable elements — now with declarative data binding instead of manual
attribute twiddling.

**Consequences**: flow math stays out of the components (pure functions in
`src/lib/energy/flows.ts`) so DP-003 ("flow direction is sacred") is enforced by
unit tests, not visual inspection.

### ADR-009: Dual Authentication Modes with Base-URL Allowlist
**Status**: Approved
**Date**: 2026-07-29 (retro-documents the proxy mode shipped 2026-03-01;
adds the allowlist requirement)

**Context**: Proxy mode (cookie auth via Caddy token injection) was implemented
in `api-client.js` and `app.js` on 2026-03-01 but never recorded in this
document. Reviewing it now surfaces a real weakness: base URLs come from URL
parameters and are validated only for an `https://` prefix, so a crafted link
could direct credentialed requests (cookies in proxy mode, Bearer headers in
bridge mode) to an arbitrary host.

**Decision**: Both auth modes are first-class and selected per-API by token
presence (token ⇒ Bearer header; no token ⇒ `credentials: 'include'`). Base
URLs **must** be validated against a build-time host allowlist — the same host
set templated into CSP `connect-src` — before any request. Validation failure is
a config error (HC-003 error panel), not a silent fallback.

**Consequences**: `src/lib/api/validate.ts` owns the allowlist; adding an API
host is a code change + CSP env change, reviewed together; the legacy
prefix-only check is recorded as a known vulnerability until the port lands (R3).

